1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,230
Heads, I riffle shuffle a sorted deck four times.

2
00:00:03,610 --> 00:00:06,340
Tails, a computer shuffles it perfectly.

3
00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:09,780
From the deck alone, how often could you tell which?

4
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:13,280
Pick fifty, seventy five, or a hundred percent.

5
00:00:15,133 --> 00:00:23,413
Any simulation that mixes by random steps faces this question: stopped too early, it still remembers where it started.

6
00:00:23,766 --> 00:00:27,236
The obvious check is the top card, the one you'd be dealt.

7
00:00:27,616 --> 00:00:31,406
Here's how often each card lands there after four riffles.

8
00:00:31,766 --> 00:00:32,876
Almost flat.

9
00:00:33,256 --> 00:00:37,116
Only the old top card stands out: one time in fifteen.

10
00:00:37,496 --> 00:00:41,606
Judging by the top card, you'd be right fifty two percent of the time.

11
00:00:41,986 --> 00:00:43,946
Four riffles look like plenty.

12
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:47,180
A guesser who sees the whole deck does far better.

13
00:00:47,560 --> 00:00:52,950
The model cuts near the middle, then drops cards from each half in proportion to its size.

14
00:00:53,300 --> 00:01:01,760
Each half stays in order, so one riffle leaves two rising sequences: runs of consecutive cards, reading left to right.

15
00:01:02,100 --> 00:01:04,020
Give each sequence its own level.

16
00:01:04,400 --> 00:01:08,920
The cut crosses each level once, so a riffle can at most split it in two.

17
00:01:09,266 --> 00:01:10,356
Three riffles,

18
00:01:11,156 --> 00:01:11,806
eight.

19
00:01:12,186 --> 00:01:13,256
Four riffles,

20
00:01:13,856 --> 00:01:16,646
at most sixteen; this deck has fourteen.

21
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:23,680
A perfect shuffle averages twenty six and a half, and gives sixteen or fewer once in two million decks.

22
00:01:24,033 --> 00:01:30,323
Call any deck with sixteen or fewer riffled, and you'd be wrong about once in four million games.

23
00:01:30,703 --> 00:01:34,643
The top card can't see this; it lives in the order between cards.

24
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:40,890
Bayer and Diaconis proved that in this model, decks with the same count are equally likely.

25
00:01:41,966 --> 00:01:44,056
So pile each deck onto its count.

26
00:01:44,800 --> 00:01:48,100
Blue is a perfect shuffle, purple is four riffles.

27
00:01:48,480 --> 00:01:52,550
The red area, purple above blue, is the distance from random.

28
00:01:52,930 --> 00:01:54,160
Right now it's one.

29
00:01:54,500 --> 00:01:56,970
How many more riffles to bring it below a half?

30
00:01:58,833 --> 00:02:00,993
A fifth riffle: point nine two.

31
00:02:01,373 --> 00:02:03,203
A sixth: point six one.

32
00:02:03,566 --> 00:02:05,996
Guess riffled wherever purple is higher.

33
00:02:06,376 --> 00:02:12,506
You catch seventy eight percent of riffled decks, but flag seventeen percent of perfect ones.

34
00:02:12,886 --> 00:02:16,556
Accuracy is a half plus half the difference: the red area.

35
00:02:16,900 --> 00:02:19,130
A seventh riffle: point three three.

36
00:02:19,466 --> 00:02:26,846
Meanwhile the gap between the centers roughly halved each riffle, while purple's spread settled to blue's: about two.

37
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:31,080
While the gap spans several spreads, the distance stays near one.

38
00:02:31,460 --> 00:02:35,140
Once it's down to about one spread, the distance halves too.

39
00:02:35,520 --> 00:02:36,630
That's the cliff.

40
00:02:36,966 --> 00:02:40,736
Gap times two to the k levels off near two hundred thirty.

41
00:02:41,936 --> 00:02:47,806
So the gap meets the spread when two to the k is about a hundred and ten: just under seven.

42
00:02:48,966 --> 00:02:51,116
Both numbers come from the deck's size.

43
00:02:52,116 --> 00:03:01,366
For n cards, the gap grows like n squared, but the spread only like root n, so the cliff sits near three halves log base two of n.

44
00:03:01,733 --> 00:03:11,343
Aldous, then Bayer and Diaconis, proved it: for fifty two cards, seven riffles is the first count that brings the distance below a half.

45
00:03:11,700 --> 00:03:13,760
Seven belongs to this yardstick.

46
00:03:14,140 --> 00:03:22,240
Trefethen and Trefethen measured information instead: no cliff, and five or six shuffles do a reasonable job.

47
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:32,450
Many Markov chains share this cliff, and samplers share the trap: one statistic can settle, like the top card, long before the whole chain has.

48
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:34,220
Back to the coin flip.

49
00:03:34,600 --> 00:03:39,760
After four riffles you'd almost never be wrong, same as with an unshuffled deck.

50
00:03:40,100 --> 00:03:41,410
Three more riffles,

51
00:03:42,610 --> 00:03:44,860
and you'd be right only two times in three.

52
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:48,110
Four riffles fool someone watching the top card.

53
00:03:48,490 --> 00:03:52,100
Fooling someone who counts sequences that well takes ten.

